1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:04,310
Five statements about space.

2
00:00:04,591 --> 00:00:12,174
Four are true, and one is a hallucination, made up by GPT and written to sound just as confident as the rest.

3
00:00:12,454 --> 00:00:14,326
Which one is fake?

4
00:00:14,607 --> 00:00:16,479
Take a moment and read them.

5
00:00:16,759 --> 00:00:19,591
I'll tell you which one I picked, and whether I was right.

6
00:00:19,871 --> 00:00:24,455
I built this game with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

7
00:00:25,255 --> 00:00:30,271
Language models state false things in exactly the same tone as true things.

8
00:00:30,551 --> 00:00:32,423
There is no warning light.

9
00:00:32,703 --> 00:00:37,335
That is the real danger of a hallucination: it doesn't look any different.

10
00:00:37,615 --> 00:00:40,735
So instead of explaining it, I wanted you to feel it.

11
00:00:41,015 --> 00:00:50,710
GPT is asked to produce five short statements on a topic, four true and one subtly false: plausible, with a wrong number, name or cause.

12
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:55,767
You choose the one you think is fake, and the app reveals the answer and a correction.

13
00:00:56,567 --> 00:00:58,582
The whole game is one prompt..

14
00:00:58,863 --> 00:01:06,567
The system prompt asks for a strict JSON object: five statements, the position of the fake one, and a one-sentence correction.

15
00:01:06,847 --> 00:01:10,782
JSON mode makes the model return something the app can parse.

16
00:01:11,062 --> 00:01:19,318
The temperature is high, one, so every round is different, and I add a random number to the prompt so the same topic doesn't repeat.

17
00:01:19,598 --> 00:01:24,183
Each round is one small call, capped at three hundred and fifty tokens.

18
00:01:24,463 --> 00:01:33,030
And this is the part that matters for later: GPT writes the fake, GPT chooses which one is fake, and GPT writes the correction.

19
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:35,470
Nothing in the app checks any of it..

20
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:37,646
The rest is a radio button.

21
00:01:37,927 --> 00:01:42,799
You click, the app compares your choice to the fake index, and updates the score.

22
00:01:43,598 --> 00:01:45,470
The statements.

23
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:50,791
The Sun contains more than ninety-nine point eight percent of the mass of the solar system.

24
00:01:51,071 --> 00:01:54,551
Titan has a dense atmosphere of nitrogen and methane.

25
00:01:54,831 --> 00:02:00,135
The Great Red Spot on Jupiter is a storm that has lasted over three hundred and fifty years.

26
00:02:00,415 --> 00:02:04,663
Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in two thousand six.

27
00:02:04,943 --> 00:02:11,327
And Venus rotates faster than Earth, completing a rotation every two hundred and forty-three Earth days.

28
00:02:11,607 --> 00:02:13,719
The last one contradicts itself.

29
00:02:13,999 --> 00:02:20,191
Two hundred and forty-three days for one rotation is much slower than Earth's one day, not faster.

30
00:02:20,471 --> 00:02:22,343
I pick that one.

31
00:02:22,622 --> 00:02:23,390
Caught it.

32
00:02:23,671 --> 00:02:26,911
The correction says Venus rotates slower than Earth.

33
00:02:27,191 --> 00:02:30,574
This one was easy, because the statement fights itself.

34
00:02:31,375 --> 00:02:33,294
I switch the topic to programming.

35
00:02:33,574 --> 00:02:37,966
Python's sleep function halts execution at millisecond precision.

36
00:02:38,246 --> 00:02:41,726
JavaScript's triple equals is a type-safe comparison.

37
00:02:42,006 --> 00:02:45,318
Git was created by Linus Torvalds for the Linux kernel.

38
00:02:45,598 --> 00:02:50,255
Fortran, the first high-level language, appeared in nineteen fifty-seven.

39
00:02:50,535 --> 00:02:54,878
And Ruby was designed to run exclusively on Unix-based systems.

40
00:02:55,158 --> 00:03:01,758
Ruby sounds the most suspicious to me, since I know it runs on Windows today, but the statement says originally.

41
00:03:02,038 --> 00:03:03,911
So I pick Ruby.

42
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:05,030
Fooled.

43
00:03:05,310 --> 00:03:07,230
The fake was the sleep statement.

44
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:12,910
The correction says Python's sleep works at second precision by default, not millisecond.

45
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:17,126
And this is where it gets interesting, because that correction is shaky too.

46
00:03:17,406 --> 00:03:23,743
Python's time dot sleep accepts a floating point number of seconds, so you can sleep for fractions of a second.

47
00:03:24,023 --> 00:03:28,103
The app showed me a fake, and a correction that is also not quite right.

48
00:03:28,383 --> 00:03:32,727
That's the thing I mentioned: GPT wrote both, and nothing checks them.

49
00:03:33,527 --> 00:03:35,399
Human body.

50
00:03:35,679 --> 00:03:38,703
The brain has about eighty-six billion neurons.

51
00:03:38,983 --> 00:03:40,855
Ears never stop growing.

52
00:03:41,134 --> 00:03:44,038
The heart beats around a hundred thousand times a day.

53
00:03:44,318 --> 00:03:48,446
Every single cell in your body is replaced every seven years.

54
00:03:48,726 --> 00:03:51,558
And humans have over six hundred lymph nodes.

55
00:03:51,838 --> 00:03:55,174
The seven-year claim is a famous myth, and I pick it.

56
00:03:55,454 --> 00:03:57,326
Correct.

57
00:03:57,606 --> 00:04:01,398
Not every cell is replaced; some neurons last a lifetime.

58
00:04:01,678 --> 00:04:03,550
Geography.

59
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:08,318
The Amazon discharges more water than the next seven rivers combined.

60
00:04:08,598 --> 00:04:12,222
Lake Superior is the largest freshwater lake by area.

61
00:04:12,502 --> 00:04:15,814
Mount Everest grows about four millimetres a year.

62
00:04:16,094 --> 00:04:19,838
The Sahara covers over nine million square kilometres.

63
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:24,894
And Russia spans three continents: Europe, Asia and North America.

64
00:04:25,174 --> 00:04:28,030
That last one is wrong, Russia spans two.

65
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:30,182
Correct again.

66
00:04:30,982 --> 00:04:33,190
The sidebar says three out of four.

67
00:04:33,470 --> 00:04:46,022
But look at how I got there: two of my three hits were statements that contradicted themselves or repeated a famous myth, and the round I lost was the one where the fake was a small, technical detail that sounded fine.

68
00:04:46,302 --> 00:04:50,286
The better the lie, the closer it is to a fact you half remember.

69
00:04:51,086 --> 00:04:55,574
That the game is hard for the reason I expected, and also for a reason I didn't.

70
00:04:55,854 --> 00:05:01,422
The expected one: a plausible detail is nearly impossible to spot without checking.

71
00:05:01,702 --> 00:05:04,918
The surprise: the app's own answer key can be wrong.

72
00:05:05,198 --> 00:05:08,462
A fake and its correction both come from the same model.

73
00:05:08,742 --> 00:05:12,294
So the lesson isn't only that GPT hallucinates.

74
00:05:12,574 --> 00:05:16,414
It's that asking GPT to check GPT doesn't fix it.

75
00:05:17,214 --> 00:05:23,334
Four rounds is a tiny sample, so my three out of four tells you nothing about how hard the game is in general.

76
00:05:23,614 --> 00:05:28,054
I used my own knowledge and didn't look anything up, and I'm not a typical player.

77
00:05:28,334 --> 00:05:36,566
The statements, the fake, and the correction all come from one model, with no fact-checking, so the app can be wrong about what's true.

78
00:05:36,846 --> 00:05:40,950
Some rounds may contain a statement that is also false by accident.

79
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:46,294
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

80
00:05:47,094 --> 00:05:55,518
Next I'd add a second step that checks each answer against a source, a difficulty setting, and a leaderboard so you can compare your score with other people.

81
00:05:55,798 --> 00:05:57,790
The code is in the description.

82
00:05:58,070 --> 00:06:01,214
It costs one small LLM call per round.

83
00:06:01,494 --> 00:06:08,166
If you were fooled by round two like I was, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which statement got you.
